// A **type is a compile-time value** (ADR-0071), which is W4's third sub-wave — and the half of it that
// adds a capability. The other half removes a silent miscompile and lives in `type-errors/063`.
//
// `Point :: struct { … }` has always been a *constant whose value is a type* (ADR-0012). What was
// missing is the one thing that follows from saying so: binding that value to another name.
//
//     Pair :: Point;      // now legal, and `Pair` is usable everywhere `Point` is
//
// Before ADR-0071 this reported `error[E0230]: compile-time evaluation failed: a file-level item has no
// value yet` — a const-eval internal, on a declaration that is perfectly correct. The cause was narrow:
// `file_consts` deliberately does not treat a struct as an evaluation target, because a struct's "value
// is a declaration rather than something to compute", so the thunk found no entry for `Point`.
//
// The fix reads the value from where it *already was*. `SigEntry::type_value` holds the resolved type of
// every nominal declaration, computed in the signature phase — which const-eval is downstream of
// (ADR-0018 §3) — so a type alias is interned from a value that exists rather than by inverting a phase.
// That is exactly the move ADR-0070 §1 made for an array length, available for the same reason.
//
// Six things here are worth checking rather than assuming, and the `exit` gives each teeth:
//
//   * an alias is usable as a **local's type**, and the local really has the aliased layout;
//   * as a **parameter type**, so a call through the alias passes the same aggregate;
//   * as a **field type** of another struct, so the alias survives one level of nesting;
//   * as an **element type** of an array, which is where a wrong stride would show;
//   * as a **pointee**, so `*Pair` and `*Point` are one type rather than two;
//   * an **enum** alias carries its members: `Shade.GREEN` is `Colour.GREEN`, the same constant.
//
// The last is the one that would fail quietly if the alias were a *new* nominal type rather than the
// same one: ADR-0015 §1 makes a struct's identity its declaration site, so an alias must not create a
// second identity. `c == Colour.GREEN` is what proves it did not — comparing across the two spellings
// would be E0214 if they were different types.
//
// What is **not** here is a chain (`B :: Pair;`), which stays refused for the reason ADR-0070 §4 refused
// a length chain: one level is a lookup, a chain needs a fixpoint and a cycle check. Nor is `Type`
// spellable as an annotation — see `type-errors/063` for why that is a decision rather than an omission.
#import "Basic";

/// The aliased struct. Two fields rather than one, so a transposed alias would put a value in the
/// wrong place instead of merely working.
Point :: struct {
    x: s64;
    y: s64;
}

/// The aliased enum, with three members so a bare `.GREEN` has somewhere to be wrong.
Colour :: enum {
    RED;
    GREEN;
    BLUE;
}

/// The alias itself. This declaration is the whole feature.
Pair :: Point;

/// And for the enum, so the member-access path is covered as well as the layout one.
Shade :: Colour;

/// The alias as a **field type**, one level of nesting deep.
Holder :: struct {
    p: Pair;
}

/// The alias as a **parameter type**, so a call passes the aggregate through the alias rather than
/// through the name it aliases.
takes :: (q: Pair) -> s64 {
    return q.x + q.y;
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // A local declared through the alias, with both fields written: a one-field struct would not
    // notice a wrong offset.
    p: Pair;
    p.x = 3;
    p.y = 4;
    if takes(p) == 7 {
        n = n + 1;
    }

    // An enum alias's member **is** the aliased enum's member — the same interned constant, not a
    // second one. Compared across the two spellings, which is what proves the alias did not create a
    // new nominal type (ADR-0015 §1).
    c := Shade.GREEN;
    if c == Colour.GREEN {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // Through a field whose type is the alias.
    h: Holder;
    h.p.x = 9;
    if h.p.x == 9 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // As an array element type, where a wrong stride would write into the neighbouring element.
    arr: [2]Pair;
    arr[1].y = 5;
    if arr[1].y == 5 {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    // Through a pointer, so `*Pair` and `*Point` are one type: `p` was declared as a `Pair` and this
    // reads the field the `Point` layout puts there.
    ptr := *p;
    if ptr.x == 3 {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // Every assertion: 31.
    if n == 31 {
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}
